Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: ARTICLE 9. Termination and Amendment. <omitted> (f) Notwithstanding anything in this Agreement to the contrary, at any time prior to the Effective Time, the Company Board may terminate this Agreement if it determines, in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, that an Acquisition Proposal constitutes a Superior Proposal. The Company must provide Parent with at least five (5) Business Days' prior written notice of its intention to terminate this Agreement to enter into a definitive agreement with respect to such Superior Proposal, and during such period, the Company shall, and shall cause its Representatives to, negotiate with Parent in good faith (to the extent Parent desires to negotiate) to enable Parent to make such adjustments to the terms and conditions of this Agreement so that the Acquisition Proposal ceases to be a Superior Proposal. After considering the results of such negotiations and any revised proposal made by Parent, the Company Board shall determine in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel and financial advisor, whether the Acquisition Proposal remains a Superior Proposal. (Page 82)\nAnswer: 
