Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is “financial point of view” the sole consideration when determining whether an offer is superior?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: SECTION 9.3. Assessment of Acquisition Proposals. <omitted> (b) Notwithstanding any provision in this Agreement, the Company Board may only change its recommendation if it determines in good faith, after consultation with its external legal counsel and financial advisors, that the failure to do so would be inconsistent with its fiduciary duties under applicable Law. (c) In evaluating an Acquisition Proposal, the Company Board must consider: (i) the strategic fit of the proposal with the Company’s long-term business goals, (ii) the likelihood of obtaining necessary regulatory approvals, (iii) the reputation and business practices of the acquiring party, (iv) the financial terms, including price and payment structure, and (v) any potential impact on the Company’s employees and other stakeholders. (d) The Company shall provide the Parent at least five Business Days’ notice of its intention to change its recommendation, during which time the Parent may propose revisions to the agreement. (Pages 93-94)\nAnswer:
